usbserial is the USB side of an Arduino Uno R3: its ATmega16U2 at 16MHz
running Arduino-usbserial, the board's USB to serial firmware, with the
USB port exported over USB/IP. Once a USB/IP client attaches it, the host
sees the Uno's serial port (2341:0043).

	make
	./obj-*/usbserial.elf

On the board, USART1 of the 16U2 goes to the ATmega328P and DTR pulls the
328P's RESET low (PD7). Here USART1 is looped back, TX to RX, so what is
written to the serial port comes back, at the baud rate the host set.
With -t (not on Windows) USART1 is connected to a pty instead, linked
from /tmp/simavr-uart1, where another program can play the 328P. Changes
of the RESET line are printed.

Other firmware for the part can be given on the command line, as .hex or
.elf; an .elf with an AVR_MCU section selects the part and clock itself.

	./obj-*/usbserial.elf -t
	./obj-*/usbserial.elf firmware.elf

Linux
-----
The client is the mainline vhci-hcd driver (USBIP_VHCI_HCD in the kernel
configuration) and the usbip tool, packaged as usbip, linux-tools or
net-misc/usbip depending on the distribution:

	sudo modprobe vhci-hcd
	usbip list -r 127.0.0.1
	sudo usbip attach -r 127.0.0.1 -b 1-1

The device then shows up in lsusb and as a /dev/ttyACM device. "sudo usbip
port" lists attached devices and "sudo usbip detach -p <port>" removes
one. Stopping usbserial detaches it too.

Windows
-------
Build simavr in MSYS2 as usual. The client is usbip-win2
(https://github.com/vadimgrn/usbip-win2, or "winget install
vadimgrn.usbip-win2"), Windows 10 1903 or later. From an administrator
prompt:

	usbip list -r 127.0.0.1
	usbip attach -r 127.0.0.1 -b 1-1

"usbip port" and "usbip detach -p <port>" work as on Linux.

Options
-------
usbserial.elf -h lists them. The server only listens on 127.0.0.1 unless
given another address with -a; "-a 0.0.0.0" lets other machines attach
the device. When the firmware disconnects from the bus, the USB/IP
connection is closed and the device has to be attached again.

usbip_check.py exercises the server without a kernel: it imports the
device, enumerates it and sends data through the loopback, the way
vhci-hcd and cdc-acm would.

The firmware
------------
Arduino-usbserial-atmega16u2-Uno-Rev3.hex is Arduino's image, unchanged.
See LICENSE.

USB/IP: https://docs.kernel.org/usb/usbip_protocol.html
